{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook with precision, tension, and emotional depth. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie transcends the typical biopic format by transforming a tech startup story into a compelling drama about ambition, betrayal, and isolation. the pacing is relentless, driven by sorkin’s razor-sharp dialogue that crackles with wit and intellectual urgency, making even courtroom scenes feel like high-stakes showdowns.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing the character’s brilliance, social awkwardness, and emotional detachment with chilling accuracy. his portrayal is not a caricature but a nuanced study of a man who builds a global phenomenon while remaining fundamentally disconnected from the people around him. justin timberlake is equally compelling as sean parker, the charismatic, chaotic force who introduces zuckerberg to the wilder side of internet culture — his performance is magnetic, layered with charm and menace.\n\nthe supporting cast, including armie hammer and jesse plemons as the winklevoss twins, is exceptional. the twins are portrayed not as mere villains but as tragic figures whose vision is co-opted and whose trust is exploited. the dual performance by hammer is a technical marvel, seamlessly blending two distinct yet identical personas.\n\njames newton howard’s score is haunting and minimalist, perfectly complementing the film’s cold, digital aesthetic. the cinematography, led by jeff cronenweth, uses cool tones and tight framing to emphasize isolation and the sterile nature of the digital world. the editing is crisp and inventive, with flashbacks and courtroom sequences interwoven to reveal the story’s moral ambiguities.\n\nwhat makes the film truly remarkable is its refusal to offer 